CAT = "caautoElectrical"; SEG = "ELE"
# RSOS 2023 MWA E: Diagnoses and repairs electrical and comfort control systems (E-14 to E-17)
Q = [
("A 12 V circuit has a total resistance of 4 ohms. How much current flows?",
 "3 A", ["48 A", "0.33 A", "16 A"],
 "Ohm's law gives current as voltage divided by resistance: 12 / 4 = 3 A. Multiplying instead gives 48, a common slip."),
("Two 6-ohm resistors are wired in parallel. What is the total resistance?",
 "3 ohms", ["12 ohms", "6 ohms", "36 ohms"],
 "Equal resistors in parallel give the value of one divided by how many there are: 6 / 2 = 3 ohms. In series they would add to 12 ohms."),
("A 12 V headlamp circuit draws 10 A. How much power does it use?",
 "120 W", ["1.2 W", "22 W", "12 W"],
 "Power equals voltage times current: 12 V x 10 A = 120 W."),
("When should a voltage drop test be done on a circuit?",
 "With the circuit on and loaded", ["With the battery disconnected", "With the fuse removed", "With the circuit switched off"],
 "Voltage drop only appears when current flows. With no load, even a badly corroded connection shows almost no drop."),
("A headlamp is dim. The ground side shows a 1.5 V drop with the lamp on. What is the fault?",
 "A high-resistance ground", ["An open in the feed wire", "A short to ground", "A failed headlamp switch"],
 "A good ground circuit drops only a small fraction of a volt. A 1.5 V drop means resistance there is stealing voltage from the lamp."),
("A circuit fuse blows as soon as the switch is turned on. What is the most likely fault?",
 "A short to ground", ["An open circuit", "High resistance", "A weak battery"],
 "A short to ground bypasses the load, so current rises far above normal and the fuse opens. An open or high resistance lowers current instead."),
("A circuit has an open between the switch and the load. What will a voltmeter read across the open?",
 "Close to battery voltage", ["Zero volts exactly", "Half of battery voltage", "A small negative reading"],
 "With no current flowing, there is no drop across the load, so nearly all source voltage appears across the gap. This is how an open is located."),
("On a standard ISO relay, which terminals are the control coil?",
 "85 and 86", ["30 and 87", "87 and 87a", "30 and 85"],
 "Terminals 85 and 86 are the coil. Terminal 30 is the common power feed, 87 the normally open output and 87a the normally closed output."),
("Why do many relays have a diode or resistor across the coil?",
 "To suppress a voltage spike", ["To raise the coil current", "To keep the contacts closed", "To set the relay's delay"],
 "When the coil switches off its collapsing magnetic field creates a high voltage spike. The diode or resistor absorbs it and protects the module that drives the relay."),
("Which wire is larger in diameter?",
 "14 AWG", ["16 AWG", "18 AWG", "20 AWG"],
 "In the American Wire Gauge system, a lower number means a thicker wire that carries more current. Metric wire is sized by cross-section in square millimetres."),
("A 12 V battery rests at 12.4 V with no load. About what is its state of charge?",
 "About 75%", ["About 100%", "About 50%", "About 25%"],
 "A healthy flooded battery at rest reads about 12.6 V when full, 12.4 V near 75% and 12.2 V near 50%. Let surface charge settle before reading."),
("In a battery load test, what is the usual minimum voltage after 15 seconds at half the CCA rating?",
 "9.6 V", ["10.5 V", "11.6 V", "8.4 V"],
 "Under a load of half the cold cranking amp rating for 15 seconds, a good battery at about 21°C stays at or above 9.6 V. The limit is lower when the battery is colder."),
("When disconnecting a vehicle battery, which cable should come off first?",
 "The negative cable", ["The positive cable", "Either cable", "Both at the same time"],
 "Removing the negative first means a wrench touching the body cannot short the positive terminal to ground. Reconnect the negative last."),
("When jump starting, where should the last booster cable connection go?",
 "An engine ground away from the battery", ["The dead battery's negative post", "The dead battery's positive post", "The booster battery's negative post"],
 "The last connection can spark, and a battery can vent hydrogen. Making it on a clean engine ground away from the battery keeps the spark away from that gas."),
("An alternator's AC ripple reads well above specification. What does this usually mean?",
 "A failed diode", ["A good alternator", "A slipping belt only", "A weak battery"],
 "The rectifier diodes turn AC into DC. When one fails, a large AC component appears in the output, which can upset modules and drain the battery."),
("A starter cranks slowly and draws well above specified current. What is a likely cause?",
 "A binding starter or engine", ["A corroded battery cable", "A worn solenoid contact", "A high-resistance ground"],
 "High current with slow cranking means something is hard to turn. High resistance in the cables or contacts would lower current instead."),
("A starter cranks slowly but draws less current than specified. What should be tested next?",
 "Voltage drop in the cables", ["Engine cranking compression", "Starter armature bushings", "Engine oil grade and level"],
 "Low current with slow cranking points to resistance limiting the flow, such as a corroded cable end or poor ground. Voltage drop testing finds it."),
("A battery goes flat overnight. What is a typical acceptable parasitic draw once modules sleep?",
 "Under about 50 mA", ["Up to about 500 mA", "Up to about 5 A", "Up to about 1 A"],
 "Most vehicles draw well under 50 mA once modules go to sleep, which can take some time after locking. Check the maker's figure and wait for sleep before measuring."),
("How does an engine coolant temperature sensor's resistance change as the engine warms?",
 "It decreases", ["It increases", "It stays the same", "It drops to zero"],
 "Most ECT sensors are negative temperature coefficient thermistors, so resistance falls as temperature rises. The PCM reads this as a falling signal voltage."),
("What kind of signal does a Hall-effect sensor produce?",
 "A digital square wave", ["An AC sine wave", "A steady DC voltage", "A resistance change only"],
 "A Hall-effect sensor switches on and off as a trigger passes, giving a square wave whose frequency follows speed. A magnetic sensor makes an AC sine wave."),
("A three-wire throttle position sensor has which three circuits?",
 "5 V reference, signal and ground", ["12 V feed, signal and ground", "Two signals and a ground", "5 V reference, 12 V and ground"],
 "A potentiometer sensor takes a 5 V reference and ground from the PCM, and its wiper returns a signal voltage that changes with throttle angle."),
("A blower motor works only on its highest speed. What is the usual cause?",
 "A failed blower resistor", ["A failed blower motor", "A blown blower fuse", "A bad high-speed relay"],
 "On many systems high speed bypasses the resistor block, while the lower speeds go through it. An open resistor leaves only high speed working."),
("Which refrigerant is mildly flammable and used in many newer vehicles?",
 "R-1234yf", ["R-134a", "R-12", "R-22"],
 "R-1234yf is classed A2L, mildly flammable, so it has its own fittings and service equipment. R-134a, used before it, is not flammable."),
("Why is a system evacuated with a vacuum pump before charging?",
 "To remove air and moisture", ["To cool the compressor", "To test the clutch coil", "To clean the condenser fins"],
 "Air raises pressures and moisture forms acids and ice in the system. A deep vacuum boils off the moisture, and a hold test checks for leaks."),
("Both high and low side pressures read high with the engine running. What should you check first?",
 "Condenser airflow and fan", ["The expansion valve screen", "The compressor reed valves", "The low-side service valve"],
 "The condenser must shed heat to the outside air. A blocked condenser or failed fan raises both pressures; an overcharge does the same and is checked next."),
("Low side pressure is high and high side pressure is low. What is the likely fault?",
 "A weak compressor", ["An overcharge", "A blocked orifice tube", "A failed condenser fan"],
 "When the compressor cannot pump, the two sides move toward each other. A restriction would make the low side low, not high."),
("Both A/C pressures read low and the air is only slightly cool. What is the likely cause?",
 "A low refrigerant charge", ["An overcharged system", "A failed condenser fan", "Too much compressor oil"],
 "With little refrigerant both sides run low and cooling is weak. Find and repair the leak before recharging to the specified weight."),
("Where is the accumulator found in an A/C system?",
 "Low side, at evaporator outlet", ["High side, before the valve", "Between compressor and condenser", "Inside the condenser"],
 "Orifice tube systems use an accumulator at the evaporator outlet to catch liquid and protect the compressor. Expansion valve systems use a receiver-drier on the high side."),
("How is a compressor clutch air gap checked?",
 "With a feeler gauge", ["With a dial bore gauge", "With an ohmmeter", "With a vacuum gauge"],
 "The gap between the clutch plate and the pulley is measured with non-magnetic feeler gauges at several points. Too wide a gap can stop the clutch pulling in."),
("A/C cooling fades after a long drive and returns after a rest. What is a likely cause?",
 "Moisture freezing inside", ["A broken compressor belt", "A failed blower resistor", "A plugged cabin filter"],
 "Moisture can freeze at the orifice or expansion valve and block flow, then thaw when the system rests. The fix is a new desiccant and a deep evacuation."),
("Water collects on the front passenger floor with the A/C in use. What is the likely cause?",
 "A blocked A/C drain", ["A leaking heater core", "A low refrigerant charge", "A failed blend door"],
 "The evaporator removes moisture from cabin air, and it must drain outside. A plugged drain lets it back up into the cabin. A heater core leak would leave coolant, not clear water."),
("The heater blows cold air but the engine is at normal temperature. What should be checked?",
 "Coolant level, blend door", ["The A/C compressor clutch", "The cabin air filter", "The evaporator drain tube"],
 "Low coolant or an air pocket can starve the heater core, and a stuck blend door can keep hot air out. A plugged heater core is the next suspect."),
("Why is a refrigerant identifier used before recovery?",
 "To protect the equipment", ["To measure system pressure", "To find the leak location", "To weigh the existing charge"],
 "Mixed, sealed or unknown refrigerant can ruin a recovery machine's stock. The identifier confirms purity first."),
("Airflow from the dash vents is weak on every blower speed. What simple item should be checked first?",
 "The cabin air filter", ["The blower motor resistor", "The compressor clutch", "The refrigerant charge"],
 "A plugged cabin filter restricts airflow at every speed. A resistor fault changes which speeds work rather than reducing airflow on all of them."),
("Why must R-134a never be vented to the air in Canada?",
 "It is against the law", ["It damages the paint", "It costs too much to replace", "It voids the warranty"],
 "Releasing HFC refrigerants such as R-134a is prohibited under environmental regulations, so technicians recover them with approved equipment. The exact rules and certification requirements vary by province."),
("A pulse-width modulated output shows a 25% duty cycle. What does this mean?",
 "On for a quarter of each cycle", ["On for 25 cycles per second", "At a quarter of full voltage", "Off for a quarter of each cycle"],
 "Duty cycle is the share of each cycle that the output is on. The frequency stays the same while the on time changes."),
]
